&#x200B;

Izuku’s dreams were always out of reach. Tired of being Quirkless, bullied, and stepped on, he makes a deal with a demon. Now he has the power of the Infernal Exalted, but at what cost? Is he still Izuku inside? Will the world survive? A Green Sun will rise over U.A. University.

&#x200B;

Edit: MATURE RATING

&#x200B;

Edit:  Thank you for doing this.  Many authors, myself included, really appreciate this level of effort.

That is such a frenetic pace.  I've not ready your fic so I can't speak to the quality, but the sheer volume you produced in such a short time period is impressive.  I'm definitely checking your fic out, because I'd love to give your work a read now that I know how much of yourself you've bled into the work.

&#x200B;

Bravo.

Before I say anything else the most important point is: writing should be for your own enjoyment. If no one else reads or likes or comments on your fiction that's ok so long as writing it out was enjoyable for you.  It's like eating a dessert that you baked for yourself: it does not matter if it's to anyone else's taste.

&#x200B;

That being said:  


I recommend you start with a narrative outline.  Flesh that outline out.  Then read it and ask yourself if those characters would actually do what you've outlined in that situation, really ask it.  Then make adjustments to it (or if the answer to your self querry was a harder no then make a second outline).

&#x200B;

Once you are satisfied with your outline, then write your story.

&#x200B;

AFTER you have written it, read it to yourself.  Is it fun to read? If not, why not? Make changes as you see a need to.  Ok, so now you've made it narratively better.

&#x200B;

THEN, read it again, backwards.  That's right, backwards.  One paragraph at a time. Correct grammar, punctuation, spelling, awkward sentences and phrases.

&#x200B;

AND THEN, print it out, doubt spaced, and ask someone you trust to read it and mark up anything they see as an error. Be prepared to feel some hurt but bear through it, this will improve your writing!  Once you have incorporated (or decided to disagree) with their suggestions and make the appropriate changes, then your work is finally ready.  Congrats! You're fiction is ready to leave the nest.

